      "content": "Thanks, that's a good start. But some of those are basically just economic proxies. I'm looking for more granular \"environmental\" indicators that might not show up in standard financial datasets. Specifically, we're trying to account for why certain areas have higher incident rates even when income levels are similar. \n\nWould things like the ethnic or racial composition of a census tract, or perhaps the density of specific cultural or religious institutions, be useful as predictive features for these hyper-local models? We're just trying to see if there are any non-obvious demographic correlations that capture the \"vibe\" or stability of a neighborhood.",
